\chapter{The big picture: choosing a model}

\section{Why ordinary regression is not enough}

Ordinary linear regression says:
\[
y_i = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_i + \varepsilon_i, \qquad
\varepsilon_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2).
\]

Read that carefully and you will spot three promises hidden in it:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{The outcome can be any number.} Positive, negative, fractional.
  \item \textbf{The spread is constant.} The same $\sigma^2$ at every value of $x$.
  \item \textbf{The average is a straight line in $x$}, with no ceiling and no floor.
\end{enumerate}

Now think about a yes/no outcome, coded 0 and 1. Promise 1 is already broken: the
outcome can only be 0 or 1, but a straight line will happily predict $1.4$ or
$-0.2$. Promise 2 is broken too: for a 0/1 outcome the variance is $p(1-p)$, which
is largest at $p=0.5$ and shrinks to zero at the edges --- so the spread
\emph{must} change with the mean.

Think about a count: number of hospital visits, number of insect bites, number of
accidents. Counts cannot be negative and cannot be fractional. And counts behave
in a very particular way: rare events are tightly bunched near zero, common events
spread out widely. The variance grows with the mean. Promise 2 breaks again.

Let us see all of this rather than argue about it.

<<why-lm-fails, fig.width=9, fig.height=3.6, out.width="\\textwidth", fig.cap="Left: a straight line fitted to a 0/1 outcome predicts impossible probabilities (shaded regions). Right: for count data the spread of the residuals fans out as the fitted value grows, so a single constant variance cannot describe it.">>=
set.seed(11)
n  <- 200
x  <- runif(n, -3, 3)
p  <- 1 / (1 + exp(-(0.2 + 1.6 * x)))          # true probability curve
y  <- rbinom(n, 1, p)                          # the 0/1 outcome we observe
cnt <- rpois(n, lambda = exp(1.1 + 0.55 * x))  # a count outcome

par(mfrow = c(1, 2)); par_book()

## ---- Panel 1: linear model on a 0/1 outcome -------------------------------
plot(x, y, pch = 21, bg = adjustcolor(PAL["primary"], 0.35), col = "white",
     cex = 1.1, ylim = c(-0.35, 1.35), xlab = "x", ylab = "y (0 or 1)",
     main = "A straight line on a yes/no outcome")
grid_book()
rect(-4, 1, 4, 1.5, col = adjustcolor(PAL["red"], 0.10), border = NA)
rect(-4, -0.5, 4, 0, col = adjustcolor(PAL["red"], 0.10), border = NA)
abline(h = c(0, 1), col = PAL["red"], lty = 3)
abline(lm(y ~ x), col = PAL["red"], lwd = 2.4)
curve(1/(1 + exp(-(0.2 + 1.6*x))), add = TRUE, col = PAL["green"], lwd = 2.4)
legend("topleft", bty = "n", cex = 0.75, lwd = 2.4,
       col = c(PAL["red"], PAL["green"]),
       legend = c("lm(): leaves [0,1]", "logistic: stays inside"))

## ---- Panel 2: constant variance fails for counts ---------------------------
m_lm <- lm(cnt ~ x)
plot(fitted(m_lm), resid(m_lm), pch = 21, bg = adjustcolor(PAL["accent"], 0.45),
     col = "white", cex = 1.1, xlab = "fitted value", ylab = "residual",
     main = "Residuals from lm() on counts")
grid_book(); abline(h = 0, col = PAL["grey"], lwd = 1.5)
lines(lowess(fitted(m_lm), abs(resid(m_lm)))$x,
      lowess(fitted(m_lm), abs(resid(m_lm)))$y, col = PAL["red"], lwd = 2.2)
lines(lowess(fitted(m_lm), -abs(resid(m_lm)))$x,
      lowess(fitted(m_lm), -abs(resid(m_lm)))$y, col = PAL["red"], lwd = 2.2)
legend("topleft", bty = "n", cex = 0.75,
       legend = "spread widens with the mean", text.col = PAL["red"])
@

\begin{keyidea}
The problem is never that the relationship is ``non-linear''. The problem is
that the outcome lives on a restricted scale and its variability is tied to its
mean. Generalised linear models fix both at once.
\end{keyidea}

\section{The decision tree}

Here is the map for the whole book. Start at the top with the question
\emph{what kind of thing is my outcome?}

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  font=\sffamily\footnotesize,
  node distance=6mm and 5mm,
  box/.style={rectangle, rounded corners=2pt, draw=rule1, fill=white,
              align=center, inner sep=4pt, minimum height=7mm, text width=30mm},
  q/.style={box, fill=primarylt, draw=primary, text width=34mm},
  m/.style={box, fill=greenlt, draw=greenbox, text width=32mm},
  lbl/.style={font=\sffamily\scriptsize\itshape, text=accent, inner sep=1.5pt},
  ar/.style={-{Stealth[length=4pt]}, draw=rule1, thick}
]
\node[q] (start) {What is the outcome?};

\node[q, below left=10mm and 22mm of start] (bin) {Yes / no\\(or $k$ out of $n$)};
\node[q, below right=10mm and 22mm of start] (cnt) {A count\\$0,1,2,3,\dots$};

\node[m, below=8mm of bin] (logit) {\textbf{Logistic regression}\\\fn{glm(family=binomial)}\\Ch.\ 3--4};
\node[q, below=8mm of logit] (binod) {Grouped data, and\\more spread than\\binomial allows?};
\node[m, below=7mm of binod] (qbin) {\textbf{Quasi-binomial} or\\\textbf{beta-binomial}\\Ch.\ 4, 8};

\node[m, below=8mm of cnt] (pois) {\textbf{Poisson regression}\\\fn{glm(family=poisson)}\\Ch.\ 5--6};
\node[q, below=8mm of pois] (od) {Is $\Var(y) > \E(y)$?\\(overdispersion)\\Ch.\ 7};
\node[m, below=7mm of od] (nb) {\textbf{Quasi-Poisson},\\\textbf{negative binomial}\\Ch.\ 8--9};
\node[q, below=7mm of nb] (zero) {Far more zeros\\than the model\\can produce?};
\node[m, below=7mm of zero] (zi) {\textbf{Zero-inflated} or\\\textbf{hurdle} model\\Ch.\ 10};

\node[q, text width=64mm] (clust) at ($(start |- zi.south) + (0,-1.5cm)$)
  {\textbf{Are the observations grouped?} Repeated measures on the same subject,
   pupils inside schools, animals inside herds, plots inside farms\dots};
\node[m, below=7mm of clust, text width=64mm, fill=amberlt, draw=amber] (glmm)
  {\textbf{Add random effects: a GLMM.}\ \fn{glmer()}, \fn{glmmTMB()} --- or use
   \textbf{GEE} if you want population-average effects. Ch.\ 14--16};

\draw[ar] (start) -| (bin);
\draw[ar] (start) -| (cnt);
\draw[ar] (bin) -- (logit);
\draw[ar] (logit) -- (binod);
\draw[ar] (binod) -- node[lbl,right] {yes} (qbin);
\draw[ar] (cnt) -- (pois);
\draw[ar] (pois) -- (od);
\draw[ar] (od) -- node[lbl,right] {yes} (nb);
\draw[ar] (nb) -- (zero);
\draw[ar] (zero) -- node[lbl,right] {yes} (zi);
\draw[ar] (qbin) |- (clust.west);
\draw[ar] (zi) |- (clust.east);
\draw[ar] (clust) -- node[lbl,right] {yes} (glmm);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\section{The same table, in words}

\begin{center}
\small\sffamily
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.24\textwidth}p{0.30\textwidth}p{0.40\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Your outcome} & \textbf{Model} & \textbf{\R{} call} \\
\midrule
Continuous, symmetric & Linear regression & \fn{lm(y \textasciitilde{} x)} \\
Yes / no & Logistic regression & \fn{glm(y \textasciitilde{} x, family=binomial)} \\
$k$ successes out of $n$ & Binomial regression & \fn{glm(cbind(k, n-k) \textasciitilde{} x, family=binomial)} \\
Proportion, overdispersed & Quasi-binomial & \fn{glm(\dots, family=quasibinomial)} \\
Count, variance $\approx$ mean & Poisson & \fn{glm(y \textasciitilde{} x, family=poisson)} \\
Count per unit time/space & Poisson with offset & \fn{glm(y \textasciitilde{} x + offset(log(t)), family=poisson)} \\
Count, variance $>$ mean & Quasi-Poisson & \fn{glm(\dots, family=quasipoisson)} \\
Count, variance $>$ mean & Negative binomial & \fn{MASS::glm.nb(y \textasciitilde{} x)} \\
Count with excess zeros & Zero-inflated & \fn{pscl::zeroinfl(y \textasciitilde{} x | z)} \\
Count, zeros are a separate process & Hurdle & \fn{pscl::hurdle(y \textasciitilde{} x | z)} \\
Any of the above, grouped data & GLMM & \fn{lme4::glmer(y \textasciitilde{} x + (1|group), \dots)} \\
Any of the above, want marginal effects & GEE & \fn{geepack::geeglm(\dots)} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\section{What the distributions actually look like}

Before fitting anything, it helps to know the shapes you are choosing between.

<<dist-shapes, fig.width=9.4, fig.height=5.6, out.width="\\textwidth", fig.cap="The four distributions this book uses. Notice that the Poisson is fully determined by its mean, while the negative binomial has a second knob that lets it be much more spread out at the same mean.">>=
par(mfrow = c(2, 3)); par_book()
bar <- function(k, prob, main, col) {
  barplot(prob, names.arg = k, col = adjustcolor(col, 0.75), border = "white",
          main = main, xlab = "y", ylab = "probability", cex.names = 0.75)
}
k <- 0:14
bar(k, dpois(k, 1),  "Poisson, mean = 1",  PAL["primary"])
bar(k, dpois(k, 4),  "Poisson, mean = 4",  PAL["primary"])
bar(k, dpois(k, 9),  "Poisson, mean = 9",  PAL["primary"])
bar(k, dbinom(k, 14, 0.3), "Binomial, n = 14, p = 0.3", PAL["green"])
bar(k, dnbinom(k, mu = 4, size = 10),  "Neg. binomial, mean = 4, theta = 10", PAL["accent"])
bar(k, dnbinom(k, mu = 4, size = 0.5), "Neg. binomial, mean = 4, theta = 0.5", PAL["accent"])
@

Three things to take away from that figure:

\begin{itemize}
  \item A Poisson with a small mean is squashed against zero and skewed right.
        With a large mean it becomes almost symmetric --- which is why for large
        counts, ordinary regression sometimes works ``well enough''.
  \item The Poisson has \emph{one} parameter. Once you say the mean is 4, the
        variance is also 4. You have no freedom left.
  \item The negative binomial has a second parameter, usually called $\theta$
        (\fn{theta}) or \fn{size}. Large $\theta$ makes it look like a Poisson.
        Small $\theta$ makes it long-tailed and full of zeros \emph{at the same
        mean}. That extra knob is the whole reason it exists.
\end{itemize}

\begin{plainenglish}[Mean and variance, side by side]
Normal: mean and variance are unrelated --- you can have mean 5 with variance 0.01
or 900. \\
Poisson: variance $=$ mean, no choice. \\
Negative binomial: variance $=$ mean $+$ mean$^2/\theta$, always bigger than the
mean. \\
Binomial: variance $= np(1-p)$, biggest at $p = 0.5$.
\end{plainenglish}

\section{Three questions to ask of any dataset}

Before you write a single \fn{glm} call, ask:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{What can this outcome physically be?} Bounded 0/1? Non-negative
        integers? Non-negative integers with an upper limit? The answer picks your
        family.
  \item \textbf{Was every observation given the same opportunity?} If some people
        were followed for two years and some for two months, you need an offset
        (Chapter 6). Forgetting this is the most common serious error in count
        regression.
  \item \textbf{Are any two rows more alike than two random rows?} Two visits by
        the same patient, two pupils in the same class, two chicks in the same
        brood. If yes, you need random effects (Chapters 14--15) or robust
        standard errors, or your $p$-values will be far too small.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{warningbox}[The most expensive mistake in this whole book]
Ignoring clustering does not usually change your estimates very much. It changes
your \emph{standard errors} --- often by a factor of two or three. That turns
$p = 0.20$ into $p = 0.02$ and produces a confident published conclusion that is
simply not supported by the data.
\end{warningbox}
